nd purify them of their their dross  their chaff all the stuff that we want  to leave behind we can take what  religion was offering and refine it  Kierkegaard is saying unless you  actually grasp faith to begin with and  that may be the work of a lifetime  you're really working off of a fake  starting point  you don't actually understand what  you're proceeding from and we see so  many people like this again to use the  example of our contemporary media just  because you read a Wikipedia article on  something or a sparknotes  or watch a video or watch you know a  documentary does not mean that you  actually understand what it is that  you're talking about with the sort of  thoroughness that would be required for  you to transcend it to go beyond it to  make progress towards something else and  there may be many things where we have  to experience that existentially  experientially throwing ourselves into  it or we really can't talk about it  competently so one key challenge for our  age getting past this illusion of taking  faith as a starting point rather than  something that we're trying to  understand and proceed to the last thing  that I want to bring up in this this set  of issues that's also connected with it  is this problem that he discusses in the  epilogue the problem of  intergenerational learning and he says  what ten generations learn from each  other it's a wonderful passage he says  whatever one generation learns from  another no generation learns the  essentially human from a previous one in  this respect each generation begins  primitively  and we'll come back to that in a moment  because we need to make a very  important distinction here if we say no  generation learns anything from the  previous generation they have to  completely begin from the point a and  move on well that's nonsense because the  whole of human culture is passing on  from one generation to the next and  inheritance that we can take up and do  something with the you know the fact  that we have books in the first place  that Kierkegaard who's been dead over a  century is available and accessible to  us here in these books is an example of  that Kierkegaard himself wrote in Danish  this is an English translation somebody  else the Hong's took up his work and  provided it for us the technology that  you're you're contacting me at a  distance through at this very moment  whether it be video or podcast or blog  or whatever it happens to be is part of  that as well  generations do provide something else to  the following generation that they can  pick up and use the key difference here  is when it comes to what tier two guard  has said is essentially human so let's  look at this passage a little bit more  he says each generation begins  primitively  has no other task other than what each  previous generation had nor does it  advance further insofar as the previous  generations did not betray the task and  deceive themselves so he uses the  example of love all of us have to learn  what it means experientially to love  does that mean that we can't benefit  from the advice of older people does  that mean that we can't look back to  Aristotle and read his classic treatise  on friendship in nikka mckay and ethics  books eight and nine and benefit from  that as well no it doesn't mean that but  it also means that we ourselves as each  generation have to figure these things  out for ourselves have  orient ourselves within them and there's  no possible substitute for that so he  goes on and he says the essentially  humanist passion in passion he says one  generation perfectly understands another  and understands it stuff and this is  where he brings up love but he also  tells us the highest passion is faith  and so we cannot learn faith just by  getting ourselves a textbook or  purchasing a course or listening to  lectures we have to work at it we have  to appropriate it we have to take  ownership of it ourselves and this is in  fact you might say a cr